1. Product Management is a Continuous Process
  - Product management does not end with launching a product. It involves continuously tracking customer feedback, measuring performance and making improvements based on changing customer needs.
  - A PM needs to evaluate whether the product is delivering the intended value and decide what needs to be improved, expanded or discontinued.

2. Focus on Customer Problems, Not Just Features
  - One of the key learnings was the importance of understanding the actual problem a customer is trying to solve rather than directly working on a requested feature.
  - The Jobs to Be Done (JTBD) framework helps identify the underlying customer need and ensures that the solution addresses the problem effectively.
  - This also highlights the importance of customer discovery before moving into solution design.

3. Product Strategy Involves Making Trade-offs
  - A product strategy is not just about deciding what to build, but also about deciding what not to build.
  - Prioritisation should consider customer impact, business value, technical feasibility and available resources.
  - A roadmap should provide direction, but it should also evolve as we learn more about customers and the market.

4. Use Data and Experimentation to Make Decisions
  - Product decisions should be supported by customer insights, data and evidence rather than assumptions.
  - Experimentation helps validate ideas on a smaller scale before investing in a wider rollout.
  - Defining success metrics upfront is important to understand whether a solution is actually delivering the expected outcome.

5. AI Products Require a Different Approach to Evaluation
  - AI-powered products need additional consideration because their outputs may not always be consistent or predictable.
  - PMs need to understand the capabilities and limitations of AI and evaluate whether it is the right solution for the customer problem.
  - Measuring output quality, accuracy and customer value is important when building and managing AI-powered products.

Takeaway

Good product management starts with identifying the right customer problem, not with building more features.** As a PM, I need to spend time understanding customer needs, evaluate different solutions and make prioritisation decisions based on customer and business impact. 
I also understood that launching a product is only one part of the journey; measuring its performance and continuously improving it are equally important.

